Just to recap: Stunning is _completely_ useless past the very early
game (once your luck is up, you hit all the time anyway).  Insight
goes up, but not measurably.  Cancellation is useless against most
creatures, and too unreliable at +2 to be significant.  The one thing
that vaguely affects the game is a 5% drop in the scare chance, but
you have 100% chance of writing Elbereth in one turn anyway--which
you'll do if you want to scare things, and affects everything around
you rather than just one thing.

Really, Magicbane's nicest effects (the athame-standard Elbereth,
curse absorption, and magic resistance) aren't changed by going from
+2 to +7.  The chance of confusion isn't changed by going from +2 to
+7.

The one thing that matters at all (scare monsters) is marginalized by
1-turn E, and your damage output goes up significantly (which protects
you by not letting things attack you for as many turns).

If someone thinks that the 5% higher chance of scaring non-E-affected
monsters is worth 4 points of damage off every hit, that's fine; it's
just not a tradeoff that most people value once they know the
situation.



